#{[17592186503676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503676 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a122c89-2594-51a0-a191-07ea14a78a1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503676 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16+{t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j & =c(i+3{t}^{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 16+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 16 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =2\\,i+(16+{2}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+24\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(2,24).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "400be170-ade2-5e44-a6d0-defdf287f3dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503679 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-2)\\,i+\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c((2t-2)i+\\,j) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+2)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j & =c(2t-2)i+c\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2=c(2t-2) \\\\ t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $t-1=c.$ Substituting $c=t-1$ into the first equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t+2 & =(t-1)(2t-2) \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+2 & =2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =({0}^{2}-2(0)+2)\\,i+(0-1)\\,j=2\\,i-\\,j, \\\\ r(2) & =({2}^{2}-2(2)+2)\\,i+(2-1)\\,j=2\\,i+\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t-{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2t+{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fb5b9b2-b9ce-5495-8ebc-1c2e3903a753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503699 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2t=0 \\\\ 4t=c \\\\ 2+2t=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=4.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2-{1}^{2}\\,,\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\,,\\,2+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=2t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a768b90b-915c-5552-9320-dfc50699b25f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503725 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [2t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j]\\cdot (2i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 2+({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2+{t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 2t({t}^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=2(0)\\,i+((0)^{2}-1)\\,j=-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k$ at the point $P(1,2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "befac87f-c42c-5c4b-8db1-52723d372dd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503739 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{t}=2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,2)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1)}}\\,j+\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+3}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the curve at the point $P(1,2,2)$ is $\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+4t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da548a97-bf75-53d6-96e0-6ed458e117e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503747 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,-1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =⟨c,-c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=c \\\\ 6t+4=-c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2{t}^{2}.$ Substituting $c=2{t}^{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+4 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+6t+4 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t+2 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=-2$ and $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3},3(-1)^{2}+4(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},-1⟩, \\\\ r(-2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-2)^{3},3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)⟩=⟨-\\frac{16}{3},4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$ and $(-\\frac{16}{3},4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
